            round_done: `Fifteen months closed`,
            r1_brief: `Monday 11 January 2027. You are head of operations at a Madrid logistics company and earn about EUR 110,000 gross a year. You have EUR 200,000 of liquid savings: EUR 30,000 in the emergency fund (six months of your expenses, about EUR 5,000 a month) and EUR 170,000 in index funds. Last night Iker Urbistondo, with whom you worked for six years, wrote: "I’m closing Reservela’s pre-seed round, EUR 300,000 at a EUR 4 million valuation. I’m EUR 50,000 short. Are you in?". You have never invested in an unlisted company. In this kind of investment more than half of the cheques are lost or barely recovered, and a few return ten or thirty times the money: those few decide the result of a portfolio. Today you decide how much money you devote to this over the next three years, where it comes from and across how many companies you spread it.`,
            r2_brief: `Monday 8 March. You have three deal files on the table. Reservela, Iker’s company: bookings for small restaurants, two founders, no revenue yet. Asientia, which comes through the Red Ágora business angel network (a fee of EUR 600 a year): software for accounting firms with EUR 22,000 of monthly recurring revenue, a lead investor who has done the due diligence, and tickets from EUR 2,000. And Estibia, an AI load-planning startup that is in a paid pilot with your own division and is bidding for a EUR 400,000 contract that you will recommend or not in June; its founder offers you a way in with your standard cheque, up to EUR 20,000, at a EUR 2 million valuation, half of what everyone else pays. Your standard cheque comes from your plan: the budget without the reserve, divided by the companies planned. Today you decide what to do about Iker, how you choose the other companies and what you answer Estibia.`,
            r3_brief: `Monday 10 May. Time to sign. Iker sends you the draft: shares at a EUR 4 million pre-money valuation, "the price the others have already accepted". For a company with no revenue, your estimate of a reasonable price is closer to EUR 2.5 million. Another investor proposes an uncapped convertible loan that converts into shares at the next round with a 10% discount. Nobody has mentioned a shareholders’ agreement yet. The Red Ágora network, for its part, offers to invest through its special-purpose vehicle, which signs for everyone and takes 10% of the gains. Today you decide the price and instrument with Iker, which rights you ask for in writing and through which vehicle you invest in the network’s deals.`,
            r4_brief: `Monday 3 April 2028. Fifteen months have passed. One of your companies closed in February. Reservela has spent almost all its cash and Iker asks the shareholders for a bridge loan: "three more months and we’ve got it". The best of your 2027 companies is raising its Series A at three times the price you paid, and you can keep your percentage. The income-tax season opens: the state deduction for investing in new or recently created companies is 50% of what you subscribed, subject to conditions, and it cannot exceed the state share of your income tax, about EUR 15,000 a year. And two years of your plan remain. Today you decide where the reserve goes, what you declare and how you carry on.`,
            brief_done: `The fifteen months are closed. Review the final summary and the history table.`,
            r1_heading: `Decision 1 · How much and how`,
            r1_intro: `They hold for the three years of the plan. Until you decide the rest, the calculation assumes a standard cheque for Iker, companies picked on instinct, a EUR 4 million price, a standard agreement and direct investment.`,
